<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf III of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1050–<a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Olaf Kyrre, Olaf the Peaceful</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, known as Olaf Kyrre ('the Peaceful'), was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> and survived his father's defeat at Stamford Bridge in 1066. King of Norway from 1067 to 1093, he kept the kingdom at peace, encouraged trade and the growth of towns, and is traditionally credited with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. His reign also strengthened the church, with fixed bishoprics in Nidaros, Bergen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-iii-of-norway.jpg" alt="Olaf III of Norway" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stenersen, L. B (1843-1921) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_Kyrre_mynt_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
